WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.240
People say AI is turning us into the

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:07.800
stupidest generation in history. I don't

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:10.000
buy it. The real danger is what I call

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.920
the crisis of critical thinking. [music]

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.520
Because in the age of AI, anything can

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:17.520
be faked, and a lie looks exactly like

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.640
the truth. That's where we need critical

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.270
thinking. For those of you who are new

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:24.440
here, I trained as a monk and then went

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:27.120
[music] to MIT, and then spent many

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:29.475
years running and advising companies as

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.600
a CEO, as a board member. And

00:00:29.475 --> 00:00:31.560
>> [music]

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.680
>> one thing I've learned is this. When

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:37.040
machines can outsmart all of us, the

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:39.160
only ability that will matter is your

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.880
own judgment. So, in this video, we'll

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:44.240
cover five distortions that hijack your

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:47.160
judgment every day, and one simple tool

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:49.120
in each case to improve how to think

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.560
[music] more clearly. Learn to see all

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.040
five, and you'll feel like a genius

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:55.840
catching what everyone else is missing.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:58.760
Let's start our story with [music] one

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:02.200
of the most expensive phone calls ever.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:04.480
In January of 2024, an employee working

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:07.120
in the finance department at the Hong

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:10.120
Kong company called Arup got a message

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.000
from his CFO about transferring $25

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.680
million,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:18.040
and he thought it smelled fishy. So, the

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:20.360
employee asked the CEO to get on a video

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.760
call to confirm it. All right, no

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:24.760
problem. The CFO got on a video

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:26.680
conference along with a few team

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:29.240
members, and they walked the finance

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.080
employee to a step-by-step process to

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:33.360
transfer the money. Now, the employee

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:36.400
was super proud of himself because he

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:39.480
insisted on in-person instructions from

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:42.200
his CFO on a video conference. So, he

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:45.560
transferred the money, and days later,

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:48.720
he and his company found out that every

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:51.280
person on that call was a deepfake. A

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.880
deepfake is a fake video or voice made

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.880
by AI to look and sound exactly like the

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:59.680
real [music] person. The faces, the

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:02.640
voices, all of it was generated by AI

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:05.280
from clips of the CFO scraped from the

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:08.039
internet. And of course, the money was

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.000
gone forever. This is a true story. This

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:12.160
is the world we live in right now. You

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.000
know, we've all heard that saying,

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:17.520
finding the needle in a haystack. But

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.680
now, you have to find the real needle

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.040
from the haystack of fake needles that

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:25.240
look exactly like the real one. And this

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.200
is exactly where critical thinking comes

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:30.320
in handy. [music] And by the way, if you

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.360
want one idea every week that helps you

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.400
think more critically, please subscribe

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:38.520
to my newsletter. Every Tuesday, one

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.880
insight, one tool, one practice.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:43.520
Link is below. It's totally free. What

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:45.520
makes it hard to find that real needle

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.080
are five distortions. Three of those

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.040
distortions come from the outside. I

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.920
call them the asks.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:56.360
We spell it ASC and it sounds like ask

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:58.400
because that's the whole point. Let's

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:00.440
start with the first test, authority,

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.600
because that's the force that can fool

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:05.680
the smartest people in the room.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:08.480
A question that nobody asked. Some of

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.400
the smartest investors threw hundreds of

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.280
millions of dollars at a company called

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:16.000
Theranos, but forgot to ask one very

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:18.239
important question. Theranos was hailed

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.840
as one of the pioneering companies that

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:23.800
could run hundreds of blood tests on a

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:25.800
single drop of blood. All the tests that

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:27.800
would typically require someone poking

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.440
your arm with a needle and taking

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:34.000
several tubes of blood. It was a

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:36.360
brilliant idea. The company raised $700

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.239
million from marquee investors. The

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:41.920
company was valued at $9 billion at its

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:44.640
peak. The founder and CEO of the company

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:47.480
was Elizabeth Holmes and she was such a

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:50.519
dynamic and charismatic woman. She

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:52.960
dropped out of Stanford at the age of 19

00:03:50.519 --> 00:03:55.080
and she would work 20-hour days and she

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:58.240
wore black turtleneck just like Steve

00:03:55.080 --> 00:04:01.000
Jobs, and she had a deep baritone voice.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:03.600
She talked like this all the time. And

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.200
people noticed that she barely blinked

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.240
when she was giving answers. I can't do

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:10.959
that. She had a sense of a noble

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:13.800
purpose. And for 12 years, she kept the

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:16.600
technology secret. She was running a

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:20.040
medical devices company that felt like a

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:22.960
tech company from Silicon Valley. Except

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:26.240
that none of it was true. The core

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:28.680
technology had never worked as promised.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.173
When a Wall Street Journal article

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:33.640
cracked the facade open, it turned out

00:04:31.173 --> 00:04:35.840
[music] the miracle machine was just a

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:38.320
box that couldn't do anything she had

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:41.160
promised. And eventually, the company

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:44.120
collapsed [music] and Elizabeth Holmes

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:47.120
was sentenced to 11 years in prison.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:49.600
That's where she is right now. So, how

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.640
does that happen? How do the sharpest

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.960
people in finance skip the basics? Let's

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.320
look at three signs where critical

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:01.160
thinking would have saved the investors.

00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:03.320
First, the halo effect. Theranos had one

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:06.360
of the most impressive [music]

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:09.200
boards on the planet. Two former

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:12.919
Secretaries of State, a retired

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:15.919
four-star general, and former CEOs from

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:18.240
major companies. Brilliant people,

00:05:15.919 --> 00:05:20.320
powerful [music] people, influential

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.120
people. But did they know anything about

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:25.760
blood chemistry or medical devices?

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:28.720
Absolutely not. But nobody asked.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:32.560
Second, the FOMO. Nobody wanted to miss

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:34.960
the next Nvidia or Apple or Google. And

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.760
Elizabeth Holmes made the investors feel

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.948
like the train was leaving the station

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:42.560
all the time. Nobody asked. And third,

00:05:39.948 --> 00:05:44.533
[music] the veil of secrecy. A reporter

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.840
asked Holmes six times how the company's

00:05:44.533 --> 00:05:48.920
[music] technology actually worked, and

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:51.738
he never got a straight answer. She

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:52.000
said, "Uh chemistry is performed

00:05:51.738 --> 00:05:55.240
>> [music]

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:58.160
>> so that a chemical reaction occurs and

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:01.320
generates a signal, which is translated

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.920
into a result." That meant almost

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:06.160
nothing. Could a real medical

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:09.120
breakthrough meant to diagnose your

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:12.960
health ever avoid any scrutiny at all?

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:16.040
Absolutely not. But nobody asked. And

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:20.160
that story keeps repeating itself.

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:23.240
Enron, FTX, WeWork, the 2008 financial

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:25.840
crisis. In every single one of those

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:28.560
cases, people replaced their own

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:29.729
judgment and let the charisma, the

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:29.960
credentials,

00:06:29.729 --> 00:06:32.640
>> [music]

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:35.240
>> and the confidence of others distort

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:37.840
their reality. And the key takeaway

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:40.320
action item here is pretty simple. The

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:45.000
first skill about critical thinking is

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:47.760
just asking a simple disarming question

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:51.120
that nobody else has bothered to ask.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:53.480
What needs to be true for this to be

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.000
real? That's the question. This is where

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:59.080
AI can help, too, by the way. But only

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:01.960
if you use it correctly. So, if someone

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:04.360
makes an impressive claim, go back to AI

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:06.960
and ask, [music] "Show me the evidence

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:09.029
that supports the claim. Show me all the

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.240
evidence that challenges it.

00:07:09.029 --> 00:07:12.400
>> [music]

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:16.480
>> What needs to be true for this to be

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:18.440
real? Theranos was a $9 billion failure

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:22.080
of critical thinking, an expensive

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:24.919
lesson in bad judgment. But the same

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:27.480
magic tricks are played by lawyers and

00:07:24.919 --> 00:07:30.640
marketers every single day all around

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:32.919
us. That's where we go next, to the land

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:34.880
of true lies. You know, big companies

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:37.000
don't lie to you anymore. They don't

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:39.000
have to. They have something better. The

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:41.200
smartest companies on Earth have

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:43.560
mastered one dark art.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:46.040
How to create true lies. Start with the

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:47.800
phone that's in your head. Now, I really

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:50.200
like Apple products, so I'm not dissing

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:51.920
on them. But, if you watch how Apple

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:54.400
talks about their products when they

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:57.320
launch them, it's a master class [music]

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:59.960
of marketing gymnastics. Today, we're

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:02.760
raising the bar with an all-new iPhone

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:04.320
that is unlike anything we have created.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:06.040
You know, when you listen to that line

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:08.400
carefully, you realize that they didn't

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:11.040
say anything. All they said was, "We've

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:13.400
created something new that we hadn't

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:16.200
created before." Well, that's what new

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:18.280
means. Those are just word noodles.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:20.280
Perfectly edible, but if you're critical

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:23.280
thinker, you won't be able to digest

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:25.919
them. Here are some verbal tricks.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:28.560
One trick is up to. The company will

00:08:25.919 --> 00:08:30.840
say, "The battery can last up to 36

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:33.440
hours." That's exactly like me saying

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:36.800
this video will have up to 1 billion

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:38.880
views. If only 12 of you show up, well,

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:42.039
I still told the truth. Another [music]

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:44.960
trick is as low as. A credit card

00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:47.040
company will offer you as low as 0%

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:49.880
interest as long as you qualify

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:52.480
perfectly and behave perfectly. Miss

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:54.640
once and it's 24%

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.360
compounding every month on everything.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:09:00.040
Another trick is the phrase starting at.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:03.640
At the time I am recording this, the

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:06.480
Tesla Model 3 is starting at $37,000 in

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:10.120
the US. But, if you want all-wheel

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:13.481
drive, well, that's actually $47,000.

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:15.880
Want faster acceleration? $55,000.

00:09:13.481 --> 00:09:19.920
[music] Oh, you want the self-driving

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:23.160
activated? Well, that's extra $1,200 per

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:25.839
year, forever. Everything we hear is

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:29.080
lawyered up, sanitized, manicured,

00:09:25.839 --> 00:09:32.720
curated, brilliantly engineered true

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:34.600
lies. So, here's one actionable move,

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:37.320
and it works on everything.

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:38.480
I call it the question mark move. It has

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:41.240
two steps.

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:43.200
First, train your ear for the shiny

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:45.960
words, the word tricks that we talked

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:48.920
about. They're just empty calories, up

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:51.480
to starting at clinically proven,

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:53.640
recommended by experts. Second, [music]

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:56.040
when you hear one of those phrases,

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:58.360
repeat that claim in your head with a

00:09:56.040 --> 00:10:01.480
question mark at the end. So, when you

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:04.560
hear up to eight times faster, change

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:07.480
that to up to eight times faster? What

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:09.320
does that even mean, exactly? [music]

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:11.000
Faster than what? Once you start

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:13.600
thinking about it, once you start

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:15.600
cutting through these true lies, you'll

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:18.240
start seeing critical gaps between the

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:21.320
promise and [music] the product. Now,

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:23.840
the next skill is trickier. Creating a

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:25.520
gap between what they're selling and

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:28.880
what you're willing to buy. Let's go

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:30.800
there next to see what everybody knows.

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:33.120
Group think stops you from thinking on

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:35.640
your own. There is a famous experiment

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:38.400
where researchers brought one real

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:40.480
participant into a room with seven

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.680
others already sitting in there.

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:46.080
Everyone was shown one line and then

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:48.480
three other comparison lines. And

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:50.120
everyone was asked which comparison line

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:52.600
matched the first [music] one.

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:55.360
The answer was obvious. There was no

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:57.120
optical illusion, there was no trick.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:59.920
Only one of those three lines matched

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:02.240
the original one. But one by one, the

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:04.440
seven people gave the wrong answer. The

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:06.142
eighth person, who was the participant,

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:08.080
ended up giving the wrong answer, too.

00:11:06.142 --> 00:11:11.080
[music] The experiment went on for a

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:13.000
long time. Three out of every four

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:15.040
participants went along with the wrong

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:17.720
group answer. But here's what the

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:19.800
participants didn't know. Those seven

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:22.200
who were sitting there were hired

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:24.920
actors. They were told to give the wrong

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:27.280
answer, confidently. The experiment was

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:30.240
never about the lines. It was about

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:31.880
whether a room full of confident people

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:34.960
could [music] make you sway your own

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:37.160
judgment on something so obvious. And

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:39.160
later on, the participants themselves

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:41.520
confessed that they had known the right

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:44.520
answer all along. They just couldn't

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:46.240
stand to be the only one in the room

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:48.480
with a different [music] viewpoint. When

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:50.200
everyone around you believes something

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:52.880
to be true, you stop [music] asking for

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:55.320
proof because a thousand people can't

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:57.888
all be wrong, can they? Turns out, they

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:57.920
can be. It happens all the time.

00:11:57.888 --> 00:11:59.960
>> [music]

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:02.080
>> Massive crowds can believe in things

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:04.720
with no basis at all. The Salem witch

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:06.760
trial, the tulip mania, the bank run

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:08.920
during the Great Depression, the dot-com

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:12.560
frenzy. The wisdom of the crowd is a

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:13.520
real concept. But so is mass hysteria

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.520
and groupthink. [music]

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:18.000
And here's another surprise from the

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:20.320
same experiment. When just one of those

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:23.400
actors started giving the correct

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:24.880
answer, most participants also started

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:27.200
giving [music] the correct answer. It

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:29.600
just took one person to disagree. That's

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:33.440
all. So, the action item is this. When

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:36.520
everyone around you agrees, go find that

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:39.600
person who disagrees. Now, how do you

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:43.240
break that spell in the age of AI? Well,

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:45.360
when everyone around you agrees, make AI

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:47.839
your voice of dissent, your devil's

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:50.080
advocate. Tell it to give you an

00:12:47.839 --> 00:12:52.253
opposite argument. Make the strongest

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:52.280
case that this consensus

00:12:52.253 --> 00:12:54.200
>> [music]

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:56.440
>> is wrong. And by the way, you don't have

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:58.760
to become the person who doubts

00:12:56.440 --> 00:13:01.680
everything. [music] Just remember that a

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:03.388
thousand people believing in something

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:05.560
doesn't automatically make it true.

00:13:03.388 --> 00:13:08.720
[music] Those were the three outside

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:12.280
distortions. But we are in an age where

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:14.760
AI is the biggest generator of ideas and

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:17.480
biggest generator of distortions. So,

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:20.040
let's go see what AI knows. MIT

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:22.200
researchers asked people to write essays

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:24.839
in two ways. One group used their own

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:28.160
brain and outside research, and the

00:13:24.839 --> 00:13:30.800
other used ChatGPT. The brains of chat

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:32.400
GPT users showed the least amount of

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:35.040
brain activity compared to the other

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:37.400
group. In just minutes after both groups

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:39.120
finished, they were asked to quote at

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:41.520
least one line from what they had

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:44.040
written. The chat GPT group couldn't

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:46.680
quote a single line of what they had

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:49.480
just created. You know, AI tools give us

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:51.680
an easy and powerful way to create

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:55.040
infinite content. Instead of thinking

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:57.720
creatively or thinking critically, we

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:59.560
outsource all of it to AI. So, in those

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:02.480
kind of situations, I have a simple

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:04.800
solution. When I'm working with AI, I

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:07.600
use two lines way more often than

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:10.000
anything else in the prompt. Be precise

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:13.000
and please verify. I even take the

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:16.680
output from chat GPT, for example, to

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:18.920
Claude or Gemini to verify it. Ask one

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:21.040
of your AI engines to review the other

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:23.960
one's work. They're just mathematical

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:26.880
beings and it is your job to constantly

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:29.560
verify their output. And now we go to

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:31.960
the fifth and the most difficult

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:33.720
critical thinking skill, fighting what

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:35.280
you want to be true. [music] There is

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:37.080
one source that you will never

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:39.320
fact-check because you trust it

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:41.320
completely, yourself. [music] We don't

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:43.600
lie to ourselves on purpose, we just

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:44.260
want something to be true so badly

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:44.640
enough

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:47.160
>> [music]

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:49.720
>> so we stop checking. Years ago,

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:52.280
I had these two friends. One day,

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:55.800
the male friend of mine sat me down over

00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:59.040
coffee and told me how deeply he was in

00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:02.720
love with my woman friend and from that

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:05.720
day on, he would not stop talking about

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:08.320
her. He truly believed that they were

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:10.240
made for each other. One day, I gave up

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:12.920
and I finally had a heart-to-heart

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:14.960
conversation with her and I told her

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:17.080
everything that I had heard. I told her

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:19.680
how deeply this guy was in love with her

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:22.640
and I confessed to her that I hadn't

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:25.520
seen such devotion anywhere else in my

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:27.840
life. And she smiled and said something

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:30.640
that I have remembered to this day. His

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:32.760
love for me is not enough of a reason

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:35.800
for me to fall in love [music] with him.

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:37.720
That was very wise. Now, what does this

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:39.280
have to do with critical thinking? It's

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:41.320
got everything to do with critical

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:44.040
thinking because we want something to be

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:45.960
true just because we want it so deeply,

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:48.920
but that doesn't [music] make it true.

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:51.640
We all get stuck in our own

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:53.280
self-deception. We believe

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:55.560
in what we want to believe. [music] The

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:58.560
most important question for the critical

00:15:55.560 --> 00:16:01.200
thinker is this. What am I refusing to

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:04.760
see because I need this story [music] to

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:07.360
be true. Today, what's real and what's

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:09.320
fake, what matters and what doesn't,

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:11.640
it's all stirred together [music]

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:14.960
so completely that you can barely tell

00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:16.120
it apart. And when the world gets this

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:18.120
foggy, [music]

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:20.800
your judgment

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:23.120
becomes that much more crucial. I think

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:25.040
the answer lies in staying close to

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:28.640
reality and

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:31.880
investing in your own judgment, [music]

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:33.760
in your own critical skills.

00:16:31.880 --> 00:16:37.720
That's what this video was about. And

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:41.160
sure, the fog won't [music] lift anytime

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:42.240
soon and it is hard to see at a

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:45.040
distance, [music]

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:48.056
but that doesn't mean that you're lost.

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:49.040
It just means you have to drive slower.

00:16:48.056 --> 00:16:53.520
[music]

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:56.560
The road can be long and it can wind,

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:57.226
but it still leads you home,

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:58.800
always.

00:16:57.226 --> 00:17:00.480
>> [music]

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:01.600
>> I'll see you next week.

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:04.240
Thank you.

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:04.240
And I love you.

